PART 4 - REAL AND HONEST DATA (places, days, prices)
=====================================================================
Goal: whatever is shown in the frontend is either REAL (fetched from a named
source, with timestamp) or clearly labelled ESTIMATE/SAMPLE. Nothing else.

4.1 DATA PROVENANCE MODEL. Every place, hotel, restaurant, price, weather
    and route object carries:
      { source: 'osm'|'wikipedia'|'wikidata'|'nominatim'|'photon'|'osrm'|
                'open-meteo'|'cost-table'|'user'|'bundled',
        fetchedAt: ISO time, confidence: 'verified'|'estimate'|'sample',
        sourceUrl?: string }
    The UI shows a small badge/tooltip on cards: "Live - OpenStreetMap,
    fetched 2 min ago", "Estimate - regional cost table 2025", "Sample data".
    Add attribution required by the licences (OpenStreetMap contributors
    (ODbL), Wikipedia text (CC BY-SA), Open-Meteo (CC BY 4.0)) in the footer
    and in an "About data" dialog.

4.2 PLACE SEARCH (what the user types)
    - Typeahead: use Photon (photon.komoot.io) or the existing Trie for
      known cities. Do NOT call public Nominatim on every keystroke (its
      usage policy forbids autocomplete). Debounce 300 ms, cancel stale
      requests, cache results.
    - On submit: geocode with Nominatim (1 req/s limiter already exists, keep
      it; send a real User-Agent + NOMINATIM_EMAIL) and show a
      DISAMBIGUATION step whenever there is more than one plausible match
      ("Paris, France" vs "Paris, Texas, USA"; "Georgia" country vs state;
      "Hyderabad" India vs Pakistan). The user must pick one. The chosen
      place stores: display name, country code, admin area, lat/lng, bbox,
      timezone (from lat/lng using a small offline tz lookup or Open-Meteo's
      returned timezone), local currency, driving side, language(s).
    - Country or region input ("Japan", "Himachal") must ask for a city/area
      (existing behaviour, keep) and propose real candidate cities with a
      one-line reason each, sourced from Wikipedia/Wikidata popularity, not
      from a hardcoded guess.
    - Typos and non-Latin input (e.g. 東京) resolve correctly via geocoder
      language parameters; cover with tests.

4.3 ATTRACTIONS
    - Sources in order: Wikipedia GeoSearch + Wikidata (name, description,
      image, official website), Overpass (tourism=attraction|museum|viewpoint|
      artwork, historic=*, leisure=park, natural=*), bundled offline list only
      as the last fallback and labelled 'bundled'.
    - For each stop store: name (local + English), coordinates, category,
      opening_hours (OSM tag, parsed with the `opening_hours` npm package),
      fee info ONLY from OSM `fee`/`charge` tags or Wikidata; otherwise
      entryFee = null and the UI shows "Entry fee: not listed" (never 0 and
      never a default 200).
    - De-duplicate by name + 150 m radius; drop stops with no name; cap
      results and rank by Wikipedia sitelinks/pageviews or OSM importance.

4.4 HOTELS AND RESTAURANTS (no fake data)
    - Delete the mock providers from the production path (keep them only for
      unit tests and the offline DEMO build, and in that case show a visible
      "DEMO DATA" ribbon on every card).
    - Real names/locations from Overpass (tourism=hotel|guest_house|hostel|
      apartment, amenity=restaurant|cafe|fast_food with cuisine, diet:*,
      opening_hours, phone, website tags).
    - Ratings/review counts: show ONLY if returned by a configured real
      provider. Define a provider interface (server/providers/types.mjs
      already exists) and implement optional adapters that need an API key
      entered in the Admin panel: Geoapify Places, Foursquare Places or
      OpenTripMap (pick at most two; free tiers). No key = no rating shown
      and no invented rating.
    - Prices: hotels/restaurants get a price BAND from the cost table
      (see 4.6), shown as "Estimated ₹X-Y per night" with the estimate
      badge. Never show a single fake exact rate. Provide a "Check live
      prices" deep link to the booking site for the dates instead of
      pretending to know the price.

4.5 DAY-BY-DAY ITINERARY (proper days, proper things per date)
    - Inputs: start date, number of days (or end date), travellers count,
      travel style (budget/mid/luxury), interests, pace (relaxed/normal/
      packed), arrival/departure times, hotel location.
    - Each day gets: real calendar date + weekday, theme (existing
      deriveDayTheme), an ordered list of stops with start-end times, a
      lunch and dinner slot (a real restaurant near the route), and travel
      legs between stops with REAL distance and duration from OSRM
      (router.project-osrm.org; fall back to haversine x 1.3 and mark it
      'estimate').
    - Feasibility rules (unit-test them): active time per day <= 9 h for
      'normal' pace; no stop scheduled when its opening_hours say closed on
      that weekday/time (move it or flag "Closed on Mondays"); at most one
      long transfer (>2 h) per day; group stops geographically with the
      existing clusterPOIs + optimizeDayRoute; account for the destination
      time zone.
    - Weather per date from Open-Meteo (free, no key): forecast if within
      16 days, otherwise show climate normals; add a "rain plan" swap for
      outdoor stops on wet days. Show sunrise/sunset for viewpoints.
    - Replace the hard-coded 09:30/12:30/15:30/18:30 slots with computed
      times.
    - Multi-city trips: split days across cities in proportion to
      attraction density; add an explicit transfer day/leg with mode and
      REAL distance; do not print flight/train prices unless a provider
      supplies them.

4.6 BUDGET AND CURRENCY (rupees vs dollars vs euros - by location and need)
    - New module server/pricing/ with data/costTable.json: for each country
      (start with the ~40 most-visited countries + all Indian states/major
      cities) give per-person-per-day cost bands in USD for three tiers
      (budget/mid/luxury) and per category: accommodation, food, local
      transport, activities, plus fuel price per litre and typical taxi
      base fare. Every row must include `source` (e.g. "Numbeo 2025",
      "World Bank ICP", "Government tourism board"), `year` and
      `retrievedAt`. If you cannot cite a source for a country, do NOT
      invent numbers: mark the country "no cost data" and the UI must say
      "Budget estimate unavailable for this country" and let the user type
      their own numbers.
    - Currency rules:
        * tripCurrency = currency of the DESTINATION country (from the
          existing COUNTRY_CURRENCY_MAP, verified with a test for every
          entry) - e.g. INR for Goa, JPY for Tokyo, EUR for Paris, USD for
          Texas.
        * homeCurrency = the user's profile setting (default INR for users
          whose locale is en-IN). Show BOTH: primary amount in trip
          currency, secondary "≈ ₹X" in home currency, with a toggle.
        * Never hardcode ₹, "INR", "inr_l", "Rupie" or a fixed fuel price.
          Format money ONLY with Intl.NumberFormat(locale, {style:
          'currency', currency}) so INR shows lakh/crore grouping
          (en-IN), USD shows $1,234.50, JPY has no decimals, etc.
        * FX: frankfurter.dev (ECB) primary; fallback open.er-api.com for
          currencies ECB does not publish (e.g. AED). Cache 24 h in the DB
          (fx_rates) and show "Rates as of <date>". If no rate is available
          show only the trip currency and say so - never fall back to a
          silent constant like 83.5.
    - Budget engine (pure functions, heavily unit-tested):
        total = sum over days of ( stay(nights, tier, travellers/rooms) +
          food(tier) + localTransport(km or tier) + activities(known fees;
          unknown fees use the tier's activity band) ) + intercity
          transport (fuel = distance/efficiency*fuelPrice for self-drive) +
          contingency 10 %.
      Output a RANGE (low-high) plus a "most likely" value, per day and per
      category, per person and per group. Recompute live when the user edits
      days, tier, travellers or currency.
    - UI: budget page with stacked bar per day, donut by category, table
      with editable overrides (user-entered values are marked 'user' and win
      over estimates), and a warning if the itinerary exceeds the user's
      stated budget with 2-3 concrete ways to reduce it.

4.7 FRESHNESS AND FAILURE HANDLING
    - Cache (places_cache) with sensible TTLs (geocode 30 d, POI 7 d, FX
      24 h, weather 3 h). Show "Live / Cached / Offline" chip.
    - If an upstream fails, degrade gracefully with an inline message
      ("Live attractions unavailable - showing offline list for Goa") - not
      an empty screen and not fake data.
    - Golden-test destinations (network mocked with recorded fixtures):
      Mumbai, Goa, Old Manali, Himachal (asks for city), Paris FR vs Paris TX
      (asks to disambiguate), Tokyo, Dubai, Bali, New York, Reykjavik, Sydney.
      Assert currency, country, distinct coordinates per stop, >= 3 stops
      per day, opening-hour conflicts flagged, no stop with price 200 default,
      budget total equals the sum of its rows, and every displayed value has
      a provenance object.
    - The free agent's chat text must be generated from the SAME numbers the
      widgets show (single source of truth: the trip object) - the existing
      test "agent final message and budget widget agree" must be extended to
      three currencies.

